{# Bundle base: the SHELL's page frame plus the sheets an incident screen needs.

   Every incident screen extends this rather than the shell directly, so the
   module's vocabulary loads only where the module renders and the crumb's shared
   prefix is written exactly once.

   THE FRAME IS THE SHELL'S (ShellBundle, in the core), not this module's. The
   trail, the page head, the area's tab strip and the flashes are the frame's
   sockets, and a module that types its own copies is a module that drifts from
   every other screen in the installation.

   THE TAB STRIP IS LEFT AT ITS DEFAULT, deliberately. Incidents is an AREA-SCOPED
   module — every route under this base sits at /areas/{uuid}/modules/incidents —
   so the area's own strip is exactly right above it, and blanking the socket
   (which an org-wide hub like the files hub does) would be this module claiming
   to be nowhere in particular.

   THE SHELL'S WIDGET SHEET IS NOT OPTIONAL. The dashboard IS a widget dashboard,
   so `.w-grid`, `.w-cell`, `.w-span-*` and the whole library chrome are drawn by
   the shell's widget stylesheet, which its own header says must be linked by the
   page rendering it. Without it the grid has nothing declaring a span and
   every widget draws one column wide.

   Ordered map → widget → incidents, weakest to strongest: this module's own
   sheet is the one allowed to decorate, and it is last.

   Symfony docs — https://symfony.com/doc/current/frontend/asset_mapper.html :
   "All bundles that have a Resources/public/ or public/ directory will
   automatically have that directory added as an asset path, using the namespace:
   bundles/<BundleName>" — and under AssetMapper those assets are automatically
   content-versioned. So public/incidents.css is served as
   bundles/uhifadhiincident/incidents.css with a digest, no assets:install. #}
{% extends '@Shell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# THE MAP STYLESHEET — the atlas's ONE map.css: the plate column, the
       imagery frame, the control stack, the floating legend and the fullscreen
       rules. It is the atlas's own asset, not a copy, and linking it here is the
       self-sufficiency this base already applies to the widget grid: an incident
       map renders its full chrome without the application having wired map.css.
       Loaded BEFORE incidents.css, which decorates and never restates it. The
       map's own stylesheet arrives with the map: the UX Map bridge imports it. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\AtlasBundle\\AtlasBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The dashboard grid's own vocabulary, from the bundle that owns it. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\ShellBundle\\ShellBundle::WIDGET_STYLESHEET')) }}">
    {# The path is the bundle's own constant, not a literal: the area overview is
       rendered by the AREA module, which links this same sheet from the
       contributor's stylesheet() — and a path typed twice is a path that
       eventually differs. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Incident\\UhifadhiIncidentBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
{% endblock %}

{# THE TRAIL'S SHARED PREFIX, said once. Every incident screen sits under the same
   three steps — the areas, this area, its modules — and only the tail differs, so
   screens fill `incident_trail_tail` and never retype the prefix. Each step is a
   link only where the installation mounted the screen it names; see
   IncidentTrailExtension. #}
{% block shell_breadcrumbs %}
    {%- set areas = incident_url('area_index') -%}
    {%- set here = incident_url('area_show', {uuid: area.uuid}) -%}
    {%- set modules = incident_url('area_modules', {uuid: area.uuid}) -%}
    {%- if areas %}<a href="{{ areas }}">areas</a>{% else %}areas{% endif %} /
    {%- if here %} <a href="{{ here }}">{{ area.name|lower }}</a>{% else %} {{ area.name|lower }}{% endif %} /
    {%- if modules %} <a href="{{ modules }}">modules</a>{% else %} modules{% endif %} /
    {%- block incident_trail_tail %} incidents{% endblock %}
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}{% block incident_page %}{% endblock %}{% endblock %}